function pz_what_is_cockatoo_care_guide() {
    ob_start(); ?>
    <p>This guide focuses on what sets cockatoo care apart from other large parrots, since cockatoos (Cacatuidae family species such as umbrella, Moluccan, and goffin's cockatoos) have some of the most demanding social needs of any commonly kept pet bird.</p>
    <p>In the wild, cockatoos live in tight flocks with near-constant physical contact between flock members, often sitting pressed against each other for long stretches. That instinct for continuous contact doesn't switch off in captivity, which is why cockatoos are widely described as needing more direct physical affection and attention than most other parrot species.</p>
    <p>Cockatoos are also commonly cited as the loudest of the widely kept pet parrot species, and they're known for a particular vulnerability to what's sometimes called cockatoo behavioral syndrome: a pattern of screaming, feather plucking, or self-mutilation that develops when their contact and stimulation needs go unmet over time.</p>
    <?php return ob_get_clean();
}

